{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Increased visibility of your carbon emissions data with AWS Customer Carbon Footprint Tool | AWS Cloud Financial Management","context":"## Increased visibility of your carbon emissions data with downloadable CSV files\n\nPreviously, the CCFT summary reports were available for download in the PDF format. However, many of you have expressed the interest to download the carbon footprint report in the CSV format, so you can ingest the data into a more comprehensive sustainability report based on your organization’s needs. Along with the change of the downloadable report format, AWS also increased the granularity of the data in the report:\n\n* **Lowered carbon reporting threshold**: AWS has lowered the carbon reporting threshold from one decimal place to three decimal places, which means the carbon emission data is now available at 0.00X metric tons of CO2e, from the previous 0.X metric tons of CO2e. This change will allow customers with smaller environments and carbon footprints less than 0.1 metric tons CO2e to finally view their carbon footprint. By reducing the visible carbon threshold to 1kg, more AWS customers will be able to begin reporting carbon footprint.\n* **Improved carbon granularity**: The CCFT user interface and prior PDF downloads provide monthly summary data by geography or by service. With the new CSV download, you can now analyze carbon data at a multi-dimensional level with month, service, AND geography. For example, you can now see your carbon emissions for Amazon EC2 usage in North America. This helps you to better identify the primary sources of your carbon footprint","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws-cloud-financial-management/increased-visibility-of-your-carbon-emissions-data-with-aws-customer-carbon-footprint-tool/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Export and visualize carbon emissions data from your AWS accounts | AWS Cloud Financial Management","context":"## Calculate aggregates by AWS Organization, Regions, and Service\n\nIf you’re signed in as a management account of AWS Organizations, the Customer Carbon Footprint Tool dashboard reports the consolidated member account data. If you’re logged in to a member account, the Customer Carbon Footprint Tool reports emission data only for this account.\n\nUnlike the CCFT console experience, the carbon emissions Data Export always provides individual account-level emissions data, regardless if it’s a payer or member account. If you want to calculate aggregates by AWS Organization, Region, or Product, start with the following query in the Athena query editor:\n\n```\nSELECT\n carbon_model_version,\n model_version,\n usage_period,\n usage_period_start,\n usage_period_end,\n SUM(total_mbm_emissions_value) AS total_mbm_emissions_value,\n total_mbm_emissions_unit,\n payer_account_id,\n product_code,\n region_code,\n location\nFROM <database>.`carbon`\nGROUP BY\n carbon_model_version,\n model_version,\n usage_period,\n usage_period_start,\n usage_period_end,\n total_mbm_emissions_unit,\n payer_account_id,\n product_code,\n region_code,\n location\n\n```\n\nIf you want to aggregate by AWS Organization, remove the `product_code`, `region_code` and `location` from the `SELECT` and the `GROUP BY` parts of the SQL statement. Likewise, if you want to aggregate by AWS region, remove the `payer_account_id`, and the `product_code`. Leave only product_code, if you want to get the emissions by service.\n\nIf you want to reuse this query, you can create a **view**, which is a virtual table that saves your query logic for reuse, by running:\n\n`CREATE [ OR REPLACE ] VIEW <database>.<view_name> AS <select query above>`","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws-cloud-financial-management/export-and-visualize-carbon-emissions-data-from-your-aws-accounts/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Carbon emissions","context":"## Permissions\n\nTo access carbon footprint data, you\nneed the IAM permission\n`sustainability:GetCarbonFootprintSummary`","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cur/latest/userguide/table-dictionary-carbon-emissions.html"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Customer Carbon Footprint Tool has new features and an updated methodology - AWS","context":"AWS has announced updates to the Customer Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT), which includes easier access to carbon emissions data, visibility into emissions by AWS Region, and an updated methodology (v2.0). The new feature allows users to export monthly data through AWS's Billing and Cost Management Data Exports service, providing carbon emissions estimates for all member accounts linked to their management account in AWS Organizations","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/04/customer-carbon-footprint-tool-updated-methodology/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS reduces publishing time for Carbon Footprint Data to 21 days or Less - AWS","context":"AWS has significantly reduced the publishing time for carbon footprint data from up to three months to 21 days or less. This improvement provides users with more timely insights into their carbon emissions and resource usage. The Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT) dashboard now offers carbon footprint data with estimates published between the 15th and 21st of the month following usage. Additionally, the CCFT dashboard maintains 38 months of data, enabling users to view their carbon usage trends over an extended period. To access the carbon footprint data, users can navigate to their carbon emissions data through the AWS Billing and Cost Management console. For further information about CCFT, users can visit the CCFT capabilities and features page, review the user guides, and explore the CCFT webpage","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/12/aws-reduces-publishing-time-carbon-footprint-data/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS launches Sustainability console for carbon emissions tracking - AWS","context":"AWS has introduced the AWS Sustainability console, a new, standalone service that enables sustainability professionals to access carbon emissions data from their AWS usage without requiring billing permissions. This service builds upon the features of the Customer Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT) in the AWS Billing console. The console provides customers with their estimated carbon emissions from using AWS, calculated using market-based and location-based methods, and available by AWS Region, service, and emissions scope (1, 2, 3). Additional capabilities include customizable visualizations, the ability to set the fiscal year start month, customizable CSV reports, and API/SDK access for seamless integration of emissions data into existing reporting workflows. The AWS Sustainability console is currently available in the US East (N. Virginia) region and provides carbon emissions data for all AWS commercial regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/aws-launches-sustainability-console/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"AWS’ Customer Carbon Footprint Tool now includes Scope 3 emissions data - AWS","context":"AWS has updated its Customer Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT) to include Scope 3 emissions data and Scope 1 natural gas and refrigerants. This expansion allows AWS customers to have a more comprehensive view of their cloud carbon footprint, covering all three industry-standard emission scopes as defined by the Greenhouse Gas Protocol. The update provides visibility into the lifecycle carbon impact of AWS usage, including emissions from manufacturing servers, powering AWS facilities, and transporting equipment to data centers. Historical data is available from January 2022, enabling organizations to track their progress and make informed decisions about their cloud strategy to meet sustainability goals. The updated CCFT data is accessible through the dashboard and AWS Billing and Cost Management Data Exports, allowing for easy integration into operational workflows, sustainability planning, and reporting processes. For more information, visit the AWS Billing and Cost Management console or read the updated methodology documentation and release notes","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/aws-customer-carbon-footprint-tool-scope-3-emissions-data/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"querying-aws-cloudwatch","skill_description":"Runs SQL queries on CloudWatch Logs data exported as Apache Iceberg tables in S3 Tables. Covers VPC Flow Logs, WAF logs, CloudFront access logs, Route 53 resolver logs, Network Firewall logs, EKS audit logs, Verified Access logs, SES logs, VPC Lattice logs, Step Functions logs, NLB access logs, S3 Server Access Logs, and 20+ other AWS vended data sources. Applies when analyzing network traffic, investigating security incidents, querying exported logs with SQL, enabling S3 Tables integration, configuring log export, correlating logs with other data, running Athena queries on the aws-cloudwatch table bucket, analyzing S3 access patterns, S3 latency investigation, S3 cost attribution, or bucket access auditing.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, s3tables, athena, glue, s3\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-cloudwatch"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Checking AWS docs on the carbon footprint summary API and its GroupBy options."}

